<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abbey of Saint-Maurice d'Agaune</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Saint-Maurice Abbey</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Abbey of Saint-Maurice d'Agaune is a monastery in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> valley of the Swiss canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valais">Valais</a>, built at the traditional site of the martyrdom of Saint Maurice and the Theban <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a>. Refounded in 515 by the Burgundian king Sigismund, it is one of the oldest continuously occupied monasteries in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In 888 the Welf Rudolf I was crowned there as the first king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burgundy">Upper Burgundy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abbey-of-saint-maurice-dagaune.jpg" alt="Abbey of Saint-Maurice d'Agaune" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paebi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kirche_Abtei_Saint-Maurice.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The abbey stands against a cliff on the road between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simplon-pass">Simplon Pass</a>, on the ruins of a Roman shrine to Mercury of the 1st century BCE in the staging-post of Agaunum. The name came from a Gaulish term meaning pointed rock, and a Roman customs post there taxed goods entering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> valley. According to Eucherius, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>, Theodore of Octodurum built a small shrine around 370 to mark the martyrdom of the Theban Legion and gathered the local hermits into a common life. From 522 or 523 the monks practised the laus perennis, psalms sung day and night by choirs in rotation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The perpetual psalmody lasted until the 9th century, when the monks were replaced by a community of canons. In the mid-9th century Hucbert, brother-in-law of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-ii">Lothair II</a>, seized the abbey; after he was killed at the Orbe river in 864, the victor, Count Conrad of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auxerre">Auxerre</a>, became its commendatory abbot. His son Rudolph I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> inherited the abbacy and made the abbey his royal residence, and his line directed it until around 1000.</p><p>In 1033 it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a>. Amadeus III, Count of Savoy, became commendatory abbot in 1103 and in 1128 installed the canons regular who still live there under the Rule of St. Augustine. Its position on a mountain pass often forced it to pay ransoms or house troops, and in 1840 Pope Gregory XVI gave it the title of the See of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a> in perpetuity.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The abbey is a territorial abbacy, belonging to no diocese, and a Swiss heritage site of national significance. Its community of some 40 canons also serves five parishes of the Diocese of Sion, and the canons run a secondary school, opened in its modern form in 1806. Excavations have found a baptistery of the 4th and 5th centuries and a series of churches built over one another; the present church dates from the 17th century and the Romanesque tower from the 11th, rebuilt in 1945 after a rockfall. Its treasury, among the richest in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, holds the Ewer of Saint-Maurice d'Agaune.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abbey of Saint-Maurice d'Agaune” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 888 the Welf Rudolf I had been crowned at the abbey of <b>Saint-Maurice</b> d’Agaune as king of Upper Burgundy, the lands between the Jura and the Pennine Alps around Lake Geneva.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-arles">Kingdom of Arles · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Fisheries on the Atlantic coast, shipbuilding and the <b>Saint-Maurice</b> ironworks added to the economy, but the colony relied heavily on the Crown.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After the deposition of Emperor Charles the Fat in 888, the Welf noble Rudolf, margrave of Transjurane Burgundy, had himself crowned king at the abbey of <b>Saint-Maurice d'Agaune</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-upper-burgundy">Kingdom of Upper Burgundy</a></li></ul></section>
